course: dictionary
lesson: 5354
title: One word, three jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: One word can have more than one meaning. The word kind can be a noun, an adjective, or part of an informal phrase. The words around it tell you which one you need.
explain: «Count each kind of coin, multiply by its value, and add all the amounts.» Here kind is a noun. A kind is a type or sort of thing, so each kind of coin means each sort of coin.
explain: «The kind words protect feelings.» Here kind is an adjective, meaning nice, caring and helpful. Be kind to others means the same thing: treat people well.
explain: «I'm kind of tired.» Kind of is informal and means a little or sort of. Check it by swapping in a little: «I'm a little tired» still makes sense, so you have found the meaning.
fact: As a noun, kind means a type or sort: «What kind of dog is it?» Its plural is kinds.
fact: As an adjective, kind means nice, caring and helpful to others. Its other forms are kinder and kindest.
fact: Kind of, in informal speech, means a little or sort of: «I'm kind of tired.»
fact: Kindness is the noun for being kind to others.
exercise: «Count each kind of coin, multiply by its value, and add all the amounts.» In this sentence, what does kind mean? | Nice and caring | A little | Sweet tasting | *A type or sort
exercise: «The kind words protect feelings.» What does kind mean here? | Cruel and loud | *Nice, caring and helpful | Quiet and tired | Sort of, a little
exercise: «I'm kind of tired.» What does kind of mean? | *A little, or sort of | Exactly the same as kind | Very angry | Kind to tired people
test: «Each kind of firefly has its own flash code.» How is kind used in this sentence? | As an adjective meaning nice | As a verb meaning to help | *As a noun meaning a type or sort | As an informal phrase meaning a little
test: Which word means the same as kind in «Be kind to others»? | Cruel | *Helpful | Rude | Careless
exam: Maya asks, «Is kind a noun or an adjective?» What is the best answer? | Only a noun, always | *It can be both, and the sentence shows which | It is neither, it is a verb | Only an adjective, always
